window.PH_BOOK_LOAD({"id":"20-01","translated":true,"translator":"AI (Claude)","sections":[{"slug":"20-01-01","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/20-01-01/","title_he":"א – מהות האמונה","title_en":"The Essence of Faith","body_en":"<p>Faith is a natural trait that exists in every person who has the breath of life in his nostrils. This trait of faith is neither intellect nor emotion; rather, it is a more fundamental and deeper trait from which intellect and emotion branch out. Intellect and emotion can explain and express faith, but they cannot invent it, for it precedes them (Rav Kook).</p><p>Through faith a person relies on his senses: that what he sees truly exists, that what he hears truly makes a sound, and that what he touches is truly tangible. Beyond this, through the trait of faith a person relies on his family, that it wants what is good for him, and on his friends, that they are truly his friends. Through the faith and trust that people have in one another, they have succeeded in cooperating and in establishing flourishing human societies.</p><p>Above this, through faith a person believes that his life has meaning, and by its power he plans his future, with hope and confidence that he will succeed in improving his life for the better. Through it he believes that there are true, absolute values worth striving for, such as the value of truth and of goodness. On a higher level, a person by his nature believes that there is a supreme and eternal power above everything that is seen, by whose power all things exist, and by whose power all values have a supreme and sacred worth. By its power, too, his personal life is bound up with eternity, and its worth is greater than the mundane and fleeting routine of his life on earth. To give expression to this faith, all human societies created religions for themselves; however, along with the benefit they brought to mankind, they also stumbled into corporealization and idolatry, whose harm is great (as explained in the next chapter).</p><p>At the revelation at Mount Sinai, the tradition of faith that Israel had received from the Patriarchs was clarified for them, and it was revealed to them that the Lord is God, that it is He who appeared to the Patriarchs, that He is all-powerful, and that He gives life to all and watches over all. On the other hand, it is impossible to describe Him by any form or image, and all that we can know of Him is only according to what has been revealed to us. That is to say, since a person naturally believes that there is a supreme power, he tries to give it a form and an image, and thereby he corporealizes it and sins through idolatry. In the sublime revelation at Mount Sinai, together with the incomprehensible intensity of the revelation, it was revealed to the people of Israel that the Lord has no form or image whatsoever, and that He is even above and beyond every definition and likeness. And everything the human heart can conceive about Him is a spark that is utterly negligible relative to what ought to be conceived, and even what ought to be conceived is as nothing compared to what He truly is (Rav Kook).<sup>1</sup> Therefore the entire Torah explains the Divine revelation to us, together with a severe warning against any trace of idolatry.</p><p>Faith includes within it love and awe. Generally these two traits are perceived as opposites, but in our relationship to God they complement each other. On the one hand, a person has an immense desire to draw close to God, for a person wants to draw close to whatever gives him life, pleasure and meaning, and all the more so does he want to draw close to the Source of everything. On the other hand, there is immense awe before the splendor of His greatness, for if we are in awe of powerful created beings, all the more so is a person in awe of the Source of all powers. So it was at the revelation at Mount Sinai, when the Lord commanded Moshe to warn the people: \"Beware of going up the mountain or touching its edge; whoever touches the mountain shall surely be put to death\" (Shemot 19:12; see also ibid. 20-24), for it was to be expected that, out of great longing for closeness to God, they would want to draw as near to the mountain as possible. But as the revelation continued and intensified, awe grew among the people, until they said to Moshe (ibid. 20:16-17): \"You speak with us and we will listen, but let not God speak with us, lest we die. And Moshe said to the people: Do not fear, for God has come in order to test (to elevate) you, and so that His awe may be upon your faces, that you not sin.\"</p>","notes_en":[{"n":1,"en":"Rav Kook, Orot, Zer'onim, \"Yissurim Memarkim\"; Shemoneh Kevatzim 1:219: \"Faith is neither intellect nor emotion, but rather the most fundamental self-revelation of the essence of the soul…\""}]},{"slug":"20-01-02","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/20-01-02/","title_he":"ב – תכונת האמונה בישראל","title_en":"The Trait of Faith in Israel","body_en":"<p>The foundation of Israel's faith lies in the desire to understand existence and all that is in it, and to be a partner with God in adding goodness to all creatures (Bereshit 18:19; Rambam, Hilkhot Avoda Zara 1:3; Orot, Orot Yisrael 1:4-5). This desire expresses the most exalted trait in man, who wants to understand the world and to make it better. With the giving of the Torah it became clear that this is the deep meaning of the \"image of God\" in man, and it became clear that through the desire to understand and to do good, a person draws close to God in the most exalted and pure manner.</p><p>As we shall learn below (2:1), all human beings have a basic trait of faith, which for most of them is understood as faith in God, who is the source of all life, and who bestows a supreme and sacred worth upon all the values that give life great and uplifting meaning. In Israel, however, the trait of faith appears with such great intensity that it moves Israel not to be satisfied with limited truth and goodness, and always spurs them to strive for progress and to give everything a deeper and eternal moral meaning. Therefore Israel are called \"believers, the children of believers\" (Shabbat 97a). Not only that, but the story of the history of the people of Israel, the Tanakh, is the story of the history of the revelation of faith in the world.</p><p>As a rule, good character traits are the basis for absorbing the faith of Israel, as the Sages said (Yevamot 79a) that three traits characterize the people of Israel: 1) they are merciful – sensitive to the suffering of others; 2) they are bashful – possessing fear of Heaven and humility; 3) they perform acts of kindness – wanting to do good to others.</p><p>On the basis of these good traits, in order to absorb the faith of Israel, four principal qualities are required: kindness and strength, humility and wisdom. These are the qualities in which the great figures of Israel excelled.</p><p>The quality of kindness (ḥesed) – which expresses the infinite desire to repair the entire world and the deep faith that this is possible. Because of this, a person possessing the quality of kindness devotes himself as much as he can to adding goodness and blessing to the world. Avraham Avinu, the father of the believers, excelled especially in the quality of kindness.</p><p>The quality of strength (gevura) – so that, like Avraham Avinu, one will be able to ask the great questions, which reach to the depths of the abyss, and even if a long time passes before he receives an answer, he will gird himself with strength and wait for it, and will not be tempted to accept partial answers that would draw him toward idolatry. Strength is also needed so that he can devote himself to repairing the world in the face of all obstacles, and even be prepared to give his life for his faith, like Avraham Avinu in Ur Kasdim, and Yitzḥak Avinu, who was bound upon the altar.</p><p>The quality of humility – so that one will be able to absorb the Divine revelation without mixing into it any personal position and without presuming to understand more than what was revealed to him. One who lacks humility, even if he has good will, will mix his personal biases into the Divine idea, and, like the idol worshippers, will mistakenly be drawn after partial ideas, and will not be able to strive for a complete repair of the world. Moshe Rabbeinu, the father of the prophets, excelled especially in the quality of humility. More on the importance of the quality of humility for faith below, section 14.</p><p>Wisdom – in order to absorb the great ideas and to refine one's understanding of every dross and error. For without wisdom, even if one is humble and good, he will not absorb faith and Torah in their purity, and will incline toward idolatry, which corporealizes and diminishes every idea. As the Sages said (Avot 2:5): \"A boor does not fear sin, nor is an ignoramus pious.\" In order for wisdom to truly lead to a genuine and precise clarification, one must cleave to honesty and truth without compromise, and dare to ask, with strength, the great and difficult questions. And even if much time passes, he should not be tempted to accept partial answers that would draw him toward idolatry; rather, he should wait until a true and precise answer takes shape in his mind, and accept it even when it is harder and more demanding, as Avraham Avinu did (below, section 4). The people of Israel continued in this path, and they did not believe even Moshe Rabbeinu absolutely until the matter was proven to them with certainty at the giving of the Torah (Shemot 4:1, 31; 19:8-9; Kuzari 1:87; below, section 8). For the seal of the Holy One, blessed be He, is truth (Yoma 69b), and therefore only by the path of truth is it possible to cleave to God.</p><p>The more one merits to develop and refine the traits and qualities required for absorbing faith, the closer one comes to the level fit for receiving the holy spirit and prophecy, as explained below (16:7; 17:4).</p>","notes_en":[]},{"slug":"20-01-03","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/20-01-03/","title_he":"ג – התשוקה לתקן את העולם","title_en":"The Yearning to Repair the World","body_en":"<p>The trait of Israelite faith includes within it the confidence that at every time and in every situation it is possible to act to advance reality, in all its components, to a better and higher state. This faith is the foundation of the wondrous optimism of the Jewish people, who, despite the suffering and afflictions they have undergone throughout history, more than all other nations, have not lost their faith in the possibility of repair. By the power of this faith, so many developers of social and scientific ideas, revolutionaries and entrepreneurs have arisen among the people of Israel.</p><p>This trait of faith finds expression in the many stories in the Tanakh and in the words of the Sages about the great figures of Israel, who excelled in the pursuit of justice and truth, in love of people, and in an especially strong desire to add goodness and blessing to the world. Such was Avraham Avinu, about whom we shall learn in the next section. About his son, Yitzḥak Avinu, it is related that he walked in his father's ways, and with great diligence sowed fields that yielded an abundant harvest, and despite the harassment of the Philistines, he continued to dig wells from which water would be drawn to sustain man and beast. Likewise Yaakov Avinu, although his employer, the wicked Lavan, deceived him, continued to work diligently and faithfully at tending the flocks, for he believed in the value of work, so as to add prosperity to the world through food and woolen garments. Likewise Yosef the righteous, the son of Yaakov Avinu, although he could have fallen into despair over his bitter fate of being sold as a slave, did not lose his vitality, and wherever he arrived he endeavored to improve the situation of those around him, until he saved the peoples of Egypt and Canaan from the disaster of famine. Jews of our own day as well, scientists and activists for the repair of society, whose main aim is to contribute to the welfare and flourishing of humanity, walk in the ways of the Patriarchs.</p><p>When Moshe went out of Pharaoh's palace and saw an Egyptian taskmaster striking a Hebrew slave, even though he knew he was endangering his life, he struck the Egyptian and saved the slave. As a result he lost his status as a prince in Egypt and was forced to flee to Midian to save his life. In Midian too, when he saw shepherds wronging the daughters of Yitro, he could not stand idly by, and at the risk of a brawl with the local people, he fought for their right to receive the turn due to them for watering their flock at the well.</p><p>Likewise Ruth the Moabite: after she was widowed from her husband, it was to be expected that she would seek a groom from among her own people and see to her future. But she decided to leave Moav and join her mother-in-law Naomi on her way back to Beit Leḥem in Yehuda, in order to support her in her terrible grief. Naomi had been one of the women of distinguished lineage in Yehuda, and now she was about to return to her homeland defeated and poor, widowed of her wealthy husband and bereaved of her two sons. Ruth felt a moral duty to accompany her mother-in-law and stand by her. Through this her heart opened to faith in God, and she converted and merited to become the great mother from whom the kingship of the House of David descended.</p>","notes_en":[]},{"slug":"20-01-04","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/20-01-04/","title_he":"ד – אברהם אבינו אבי המאמינים","title_en":"Avraham Avinu, Father of the Believers","body_en":"<p>From his early childhood, Avraham Avinu wondered and asked who created the world, who governs it and what its purpose is. All the people he knew worshipped idols, but the young Avraham, in a gradual process, began to reject them. At first he understood that there was nothing to the statues themselves, but he examined the claim that perhaps they represent higher forces, such as the sun and the moon. But as he continued to contemplate, he understood that the higher forces are also limited, and therefore there must be a Creator for them whose power is greater than theirs. Thus, out of the quest for truth that burned within him, he would not accept any answer that did not give a complete and logical response to his questions. At the same time, out of an infinite yearning to discover truth and goodness, he did not despair of seeking an answer, until the Lord appeared to him and said to him, \"I am the Creator of the world.\" The Sages said (Bereshit Rabba 39:1): \"This may be compared to a man who was traveling from place to place and saw a birah doleket (an illuminated palace). He said: Could it be that this palace has no master?! The owner of the palace looked out at him and said to him: I am the owner of the palace. So too, because Avraham Avinu would say: Could it be that this world has no master?! The Holy One, blessed be He, looked out at him and said to him: I am the Master of the world.\"</p><p>The Sages said that because of his willingness to stand against everyone, to reject the idols and to call out in the name of the Lord, he was called \"Avraham the Ivri (Hebrew),\" for \"the whole world was on one side (ever) and he was on the other side\" (Bereshit Rabba 42:8). The Sages related (Bereshit Rabba 38:13) that Teraḥ, Avraham's father, was an idol worshipper who made his living by selling statues. Once he set out on a journey and seated his son Avraham to sell in his place. A man came to buy a statue. The young Avraham asked him: How old are you? He replied: About sixty. He said to him: Woe to a man of sixty who wants to bow down to a statue one day old! The man was ashamed and left. A woman came with a bowl of fine flour in her hand and placed it before the statues. After she left, Avraham took a stick and broke all the statues except for the largest one, in whose hand he placed the stick. When his father came, he cried out and asked: Who broke the statues?! Avraham said to him: I will not hide it from you. A woman came and placed a bowl of fine flour before the statues, and they began quarreling, this one saying \"I will eat first\" and that one saying \"I will eat first.\" The largest of them rose up, took the stick and broke them. His father said to him: Why are you mocking me? Surely they do not know that food was brought to them! Avraham said to him: \"Do your ears not hear what your mouth is saying?!\" This is just what I have already said, that there is nothing to them. But Teraḥ, who believed in idols, took Avraham and handed him over to King Nimrod so that he would force him to worship the idols. When Nimrod saw that Avraham rejected the statues, he said to him: Let us bow down to fire. Avraham answered him: If so, let us bow down to water, which extinguishes fire. Nimrod agreed and said: Let us bow down to water. Avraham said to him: It is better to bow down to the cloud, from which the water comes. Nimrod agreed to bow down to the cloud. Avraham said to him: Let us bow down to the wind, which scatters the clouds. Nimrod agreed to bow down to the wind. Avraham said to him: Let us bow down to man, who withstands the wind. Nimrod grew angry and said to him: You talk too much. \"I bow down only to fire (ur). I will cast you into it, and let the God to whom you bow come and save you from it.\" He cast him into the fiery furnace, and a miracle was performed for Avraham, and he came out alive.<sup>2</sup></p><p>Unlike the idol worshippers, whose worship was mainly intended to satisfy their desires and increase their own honor at the expense of others, Avraham Avinu loved people, and his whole desire was to do good to them. Therefore he opened his tent to every guest, and even during his illness, on an especially hot day, he went out to the entrance of his tent to see whether perhaps a guest might happen by. He also pleaded for mercy for the wicked people of Sedom and Amora, for he hoped they would repent. Out of love of God and love of people, he brought many close to faith in God and to good deeds, and many of them even converted (Bereshit Rabba 39:14; Avot De-Rabbi Natan 12). And his disciples possessed good character traits: \"a good eye, a humble spirit and a lowly soul\" (Avot 5:19).</p>","notes_en":[{"n":2,"en":"In Nedarim 32a it is explained that Avraham began to believe in God already from the age of three. Some say, in Bereshit Rabba 30:8; 64:4; 95:3, that it was from the age of forty-eight. And in Avoda Zara 9a they said that he began teaching Torah at the age of fifty-two. The Rambam wrote (Hilkhot Avoda Zara 1:3) that he recognized his Creator at the age of forty, perhaps according to a textual version that he had. What emerges from the words of the Sages as a whole is that Avraham's attainment in faith grew over the years. As the Kesef Mishneh wrote there in the name of the Ramakh: \"It is possible to uphold both this and that, for he was three years old when he began to think and to roam in his thoughts to recognize his Creator, but when he was forty he completed his recognition of Him…\""}]},{"slug":"20-01-05","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/20-01-05/","title_he":"ה – הגילוי האלוהי הוא תוכן האמונה","title_en":"The Divine Revelation Is the Content of Faith","body_en":"<p>The trait of faith, which includes within it the good character traits (as explained above, sections 1-3), is the vessel for absorbing the faith of Israel; but the content of the faith of Israel is the Divine revelation that God revealed to Israel. This is the uniqueness of the faith of Israel: it does not begin with man, who creates the image of God out of his own spirit, as all the various religions arose (below, 2:1-3); rather, it is founded upon the Divine revelation. That is to say, Israel's \"trait of faith\" does not define the content of the faith, but rather makes it possible to absorb the Divine revelation, which is the \"content of the faith.\" The Torah is the totality of the Divine revelation that God revealed to the Patriarchs and to the prophets, foremost among them Moshe Rabbeinu, the father of all the prophets.</p><p>Since the faith of Israel is based on the Divine revelation, the Lord said to Israel at the revelation at Mount Sinai, at the opening of the Ten Commandments (Shemot 20:2): \"I am the Lord your God, who brought you out of the land of Egypt, out of the house of bondage,\" and He did not say, \"I am the Lord, Creator of heaven and earth,\" for God is revealed through creation in a hidden manner, whereas the Exodus from Egypt, with its signs and wonders, was experienced by all of Israel openly and clearly. So too Rabbi Yehuda Halevi explained in the Kuzari (1:11-25) that the faith of Israel is based on God's revelation to Israel.</p><p>To highlight the fact that the faith of Israel begins with the Divine revelation and not with human understanding, the Torah begins the story of Avraham Avinu with the Divine revelation to him, as it is stated (Bereshit 12:1): \"And the Lord said to Avram: Go forth from your land, and from your birthplace, and from your father's house, to the land that I will show you.\" The Torah could have begun by telling of the greatness of Avraham Avinu, who with great wisdom asked and investigated after the Creator of the world, and gave his life for his faith, and even when he was cast into the fiery furnace, refused to worship idols and to deny God. Likewise it could have related that Avraham Avinu called out in the name of the Lord and converted many people who followed in his footsteps (Bereshit Rabba 39:14). But the Torah did not relate this, in order to emphasize that faith begins with the fact that God chose to reveal Himself to Avraham, and not with man's choice (Maharal, Netzaḥ Yisrael 11). As it is stated (Neḥemya 9:7): \"You are the Lord God, who chose Avram, and brought him out of Ur Kasdim, and made his name Avraham.\" And it is stated (Devarim 7:6): \"The Lord your God has chosen you to be His treasured people.\" For the traits suited to absorbing faith that exist in Israel are also from God, for God created the souls of Israel with an endless desire to understand the world and repair it, as it is stated (Yeshayahu 43:21): \"This people I formed for Myself; they shall recount My praise.\" The choice that stands before Israel is whether to be faithful to their inner nature, to seek the word of God and to bring His blessing to the world, or to stray after idols and the evil inclination and be punished. But Israel has no choice to change their natural trait or God's choice to bring blessing to the world through them.</p>","notes_en":[]},{"slug":"20-01-06","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/20-01-06/","title_he":"ו – ייעודו של עם ישראל להביא ברכה לכל העולם","title_en":"The Destiny of the People of Israel to Bring Blessing to the Whole World","body_en":"<p>As we have learned, the Torah is the Divine revelation to Israel. The first principle revealed in the Torah, in the story of creation, is that God created the world in order to do good, and therefore about every stage of creation it is said, \"And God saw that it was good,\" and at the conclusion of creation it is said (Bereshit 1:31): \"And God saw all that He had made, and behold, it was very good.\" It is likewise stated (Tehillim 145:9, 16): \"The Lord is good to all… You open Your hand and satisfy the desire of every living thing.\" God further revealed to us in the Torah that He created man in the image of God, as it is stated (Bereshit 1:27): \"And God created man in His image; in the image of God He created him.\" Thereby God granted man the privilege of being His partner in sustaining the world and perfecting it, as it is stated (ibid. 2:15): \"And the Lord God took the man and placed him in the Garden of Eden, to work it and to guard it.\" But Adam sinned and was punished and banished from the Garden of Eden. Afterward, too, human beings sinned and were punished with the Flood, and only Noaḥ, who \"was a righteous man, perfect in his generations\" (Bereshit 6:9), did God save, and through him humanity and all living things continued.</p><p>And although even before Avraham Avinu there were great righteous people who yearned for closeness to God and for purity of character and deeds, there was not yet anyone who sought, as Avraham Avinu did, to understand everything and even to be a partner with God in bringing His word and His blessing to the world. Therefore God did not make a covenant with them to be His partners in repairing the world, and did not instruct them in the way to bring His blessing to the world.</p><p>When Avraham Avinu's preparation was complete, God appeared to him and commanded him to go up to the Land in order to establish there a people that would bring His blessing to the whole world, as it is stated (Bereshit 12:1-3): \"And the Lord said to Avram: Go forth from your land, and from your birthplace, and from your father's house, to the land that I will show you. And I will make you a great nation, and I will bless you, and make your name great, and you shall be a blessing. And I will bless those who bless you, and the one who curses you I will curse, and all the families of the earth shall be blessed through you.\" God even made a covenant with Avraham about this, the Covenant between the Parts (ibid. 15:1-21), in which He again blessed Avraham that his descendants would multiply like the stars of the heavens and would inherit the Land of Israel \"from the river of Egypt to the great river, the river Perat.\" After Avraham Avinu had absorbed the atmosphere of the Land of Israel for many years, God appeared to him again and commanded him regarding the covenant of circumcision, which expresses the eternal covenant He made with him and with his descendants, as it is stated (ibid. 17:1-8): \"And the Lord appeared to Avram and said to him: I am El Shaddai; walk before Me and be perfect. And I will place My covenant between Me and you, and I will multiply you exceedingly… and you shall be the father of a multitude of nations. And your name shall no longer be called Avram, but your name shall be Avraham, for I have made you the father of a multitude of nations. And I will make you exceedingly fruitful, and I will make nations of you, and kings shall come forth from you…\" And likewise regarding Sara (ibid. 17:15-16): \"And God said to Avraham: As for Sarai your wife, you shall not call her name Sarai, for Sara is her name. And I will bless her, and I will also give you a son from her; and I will bless her, and she shall become nations; kings of peoples shall come from her.\" Since Avraham had become a partner in bringing God's blessing to the world, God made him a partner in the plan to punish the wicked people of Sedom, as it is stated (Bereshit 18:17-19): \"And the Lord said: Shall I hide from Avraham what I am doing?! And Avraham shall surely become a great and mighty nation, and all the nations of the earth shall be blessed through him. For I have known him, in order that he may command his children and his household after him, that they keep the way of the Lord, to do righteousness and justice…\"</p><p>The content of the Divine revelation to Yitzḥak Avinu as well concerned the people, the Land and the destiny to bring blessing to the whole world, as it is stated (ibid. 26:2-4): \"And the Lord appeared to him and said… Sojourn in this land, and I will be with you and bless you, for to you and to your descendants I will give all these lands, and I will fulfill the oath that I swore to Avraham your father. And I will multiply your descendants like the stars of the heavens, and I will give your descendants all these lands, and all the nations of the earth shall be blessed through your descendants…\"</p><p>And such is the content of the Divine revelation to Yaakov Avinu, as it is stated (ibid. 28:13-14): \"And behold, the Lord stood over him and said: I am the Lord, the God of Avraham your father and the God of Yitzḥak; the land on which you lie, to you will I give it and to your descendants. And your descendants shall be like the dust of the earth, and you shall spread out to the west and to the east, to the north and to the south, and all the families of the earth shall be blessed through you and through your descendants.\"<sup>3</sup></p>","notes_en":[{"n":3,"en":"God appeared to the Patriarchs 16 times, and all the revelations deal with the people of Israel or with the Land of Israel or with both. In 5 revelations the destiny of Israel to bring blessing to all the families of the earth is mentioned (Bereshit 12:3; 18:18; 22:18; 26:4; 28:15 [?]), and in 2 more by allusion (ibid. 17:3-5; ibid. 17:15-16). In 9 of them a promise was made about inheriting the Land (ibid. 12:1; 12:7; 13:14-17; 15:18; 17:8; 22:17; 26:3; 28:13; 35:12), and in 3 more instructions were given concerning settling the Land (ibid. 21:12; 31:3; 31:11-13). In 11 revelations a promise was made that the descendants of Israel would multiply (ibid. 12:2; 13:16; 15:5; 17:2-6; 18:18; 22:17; 26:4; 26:24; 28:14; 35:11; 46:3), and in one more by allusion (ibid. 21:12-13)."}]},{"slug":"20-01-07","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/20-01-07/","title_he":"ז – מעמד הר סיני","title_en":"The Revelation at Mount Sinai","body_en":"<p>After the people of Israel left Egypt and reached Mount Sinai, God instructed Moshe to ask Israel whether they wished to accept the Torah and the mitzvot, and to be to Him \"a treasure among all the peoples\" and \"a kingdom of priests and a holy nation\" (Shemot 19:5-6). \"And all the people answered together and said: All that the Lord has spoken we will do\" (ibid. 19:8), and they added: \"All that the Lord has spoken we will do and we will hear\" (ibid. 24:7). Following their consent, Moshe instructed them to sanctify themselves in preparation for receiving the Torah.</p><p>\"And it came to pass on the third day, when it was morning, that there were thunders and lightnings and a heavy cloud upon the mountain, and the sound of the shofar exceedingly loud, and all the people in the camp trembled. And Moshe brought the people out of the camp to meet God, and they stood at the foot of the mountain. And Mount Sinai was all in smoke, because the Lord had descended upon it in fire; and its smoke went up like the smoke of a furnace, and the whole mountain trembled greatly. And the sound of the shofar grew louder and louder; Moshe would speak, and God would answer him with a voice. And the Lord descended upon Mount Sinai, to the top of the mountain, and the Lord called Moshe to the top of the mountain, and Moshe went up\" (Shemot 19:16-20). Because of the great intensity of the Divine revelation, all the senses of Israel merged, until they saw the sounds and heard God calling to Moshe Rabbeinu, and they heard all Ten Commandments at once in a single Divine utterance. Afterward they heard the first two commandments in detail (below 16, note 7).</p><p>Because of the great Divine illumination that flowed upon them, the impurity that had entered mankind as a result of the sin of Adam, and had caused death to appear in the world, departed from the Children of Israel. But after they sinned with the Golden Calf, the impurity entered them once again, and with it the decree of death (Avoda Zara 5a).</p><p>Out of great dread at the intensity of the revelation, \"they said to Moshe: You speak with us and we will listen, but let not God speak with us, lest we die\" (Shemot 20:16). And God replied that they had spoken well in what they asked, for they thereby expressed their quality of humility, since continuing to receive the revelation was indeed beyond their strength. Therefore God gave the rest of the Torah to Israel through Moshe Rabbeinu, and He also promised Israel that He would continue to guide them through His prophets, as it is stated (Devarim 18:18): \"I will raise up for them a prophet from among their brethren like you, and I will put My words in his mouth, and he shall speak to them all that I command him.\" For about a thousand years prophecy existed in Israel, and it expressed the unique quality (segula) of Israel, as explained below (16:4-5).</p><p>The Sages said that all the souls of Israel destined to be born in the future were also at Mount Sinai and absorbed the foundations of faith and Torah. This is what Moshe Rabbeinu said forty years later to the children of those who had stood at Mount Sinai: \"The Lord our God made a covenant with us at Ḥorev. Not with our fathers did the Lord make this covenant, but with us, we who are all here alive this day\" (Devarim 5:2-3). The Sages further said that even the root of the souls of converts took part in the revelation at Mount Sinai (Shabbat 146a). Likewise, Moshe Rabbeinu, before his death, made a covenant with all of Israel and with all their descendants who would live in the future, as it is stated (Devarim 29:13-14): \"And not with you alone do I make this covenant and this oath, but with whoever is standing here with us today before the Lord our God, and with whoever is not here with us today.\" Therefore, when parents educate their children in Torah and mitzvot, they are not teaching them something new, but rather awakening what has been hidden in their souls since the revelation at Mount Sinai.</p>","notes_en":[]},{"slug":"20-01-08","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/20-01-08/","title_he":"ח – מעמד הר סיני יסוד האמונה","title_en":"The Revelation at Mount Sinai: The Foundation of Faith","body_en":"<p>The revelation at Mount Sinai, the like of which never occurred, is the firm foundation of the faith of Israel, as it is stated (Shemot 20:19): \"And the Lord said to Moshe: Thus shall you say to the Children of Israel: You have seen that I spoke with you from heaven.\" And it is likewise stated (Devarim 4:32-40): \"For ask now of the days that are past, which were before you, since the day that God created man upon the earth, and from one end of the heavens to the other: has there been anything like this great thing, or has anything like it been heard? Has a people ever heard the voice of God speaking from the midst of the fire, as you have heard, and lived? Or has God ever attempted to come and take for Himself a nation from the midst of another nation, by trials, by signs and by wonders, and by war, and by a mighty hand and an outstretched arm, and by great terrors, according to all that the Lord your God did for you in Egypt before your eyes? You were shown, so that you might know that the Lord is God; there is none else besides Him. From heaven He made you hear His voice, to instruct you, and on earth He showed you His great fire, and you heard His words from the midst of the fire… Know therefore this day, and take it to your heart, that the Lord is God in the heavens above and on the earth below; there is none else. And you shall keep His statutes and His commandments, which I command you this day, that it may go well with you and with your children after you, and that you may prolong your days upon the land that the Lord your God gives you, for all time.\"</p><p>We do not know exactly how Israel saw the sounds and how they heard the Divine speech, but one thing is clear: at the revelation at Mount Sinai it was verified for all of Israel with complete certainty that the Lord is the source of all powers, and that there is none else besides Him. That is to say, after the Divine revelation that the people of Israel merited, no more doubts remained in the hearts of the people that the One revealing Himself to them is the Lord, Creator of the world and its Ruler, He who struck the Egyptians with ten plagues and split the Sea of Reeds for Israel to save them from the Egyptians, and He who appeared to them at Sinai and gave them His Torah and His mitzvot (Kuzari 1:87-91; above, section 5).</p><p>This revelation was unique in the history of mankind; before it and after it there was none like it. The founders of the various religions persuaded their believers through testimonies about a revelation to single individuals, and through arguments about the benefit that would come to them from the religion. But Israel accepted faith in God and His Torah because they themselves, all of them together, experienced the Divine revelation at Mount Sinai. The testimony of the founders of the various religions can be criticized, lest their testimony stem from a particular psychological state or from ulterior motives. But the Divine revelation to the people of Israel was a revelation to an entire people, without mediation or interpretation, and therefore it cannot be doubted.</p><p>It was further revealed to Israel at the revelation at Mount Sinai with complete certainty that Moshe Rabbeinu is God's messenger to give the Torah to Israel, as was said to Moshe before the revelation at Mount Sinai (Shemot 19:9): \"Behold, I am coming to you in the thickness of the cloud, so that the people may hear when I speak with you, and may also believe in you forever\" (below 16:9-10).<sup>4</sup></p><p>Moreover, the revelation at Mount Sinai took place within a continuum of historical events that happened to an entire people. These events began with the Exodus from Egypt through ten plagues and the splitting of the Sea of Reeds, and continued over forty years of wandering in the wilderness until the entry into the Land of Israel. This was not a marginal and negligible event in the history of a nation, but revolutionary events that brought about the establishment of the Israelite nation. There is no possibility of inventing such a fiction and, at some point in time, persuading an entire people to believe it, to the extent that they would be obligated to keep the Torah and pass it on to their children. Had someone tried to tell a people that their ancestors had undergone such formative and miraculous events, without the parents in each and every family having passed this tradition on to their children generation after generation, people would have risen up against him and refuted his words.</p><p>Since the revelation at Mount Sinai is the foundation of faith and Torah, we were commanded to remember it always and to pass it on to all generations, as it is stated (Devarim 4:9-10): \"Only take heed for yourself, and guard your soul diligently, lest you forget the things that your eyes saw, and lest they depart from your heart all the days of your life; and make them known to your children and to your children's children: the day that you stood before the Lord your God at Ḥorev, when the Lord said to me: Assemble the people for Me, and I will make them hear My words, that they may learn to fear Me all the days that they live upon the earth, and that they may teach their children.\" This is the foundation of the tradition of Israel, which passes from generation to generation until our day (see below, note 13, on the mitzva of studying faith).</p>","notes_en":[{"n":4,"en":"The Rambam wrote (Hilkhot Yesodei Ha-Torah 8:1): \"Israel did not believe in Moshe Rabbeinu because of the signs that he performed, for one who believes on the basis of signs has a doubt in his heart, since it is possible that the sign was performed by sleight of hand and sorcery… And by what did they believe in him? By the revelation at Mount Sinai, where our own eyes saw, and not a stranger's, and our own ears heard, and not another's, the fire and the sounds and the torches, and he approached the thick darkness and the voice spoke to him while we heard… And from where do we know that the revelation at Mount Sinai alone is the proof of his prophecy, that it is true and without flaw? For it is stated (Shemot 19:9): 'Behold, I am coming to you in the thickness of the cloud, so that the people may hear when I speak with you, and may also believe in you forever.' This implies that before this they did not believe in him with a faith that would stand forever, but with a faith that was followed by reflection and second thoughts.\""}]},{"slug":"20-01-09","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/20-01-09/","title_he":"ט – מתן תורה","title_en":"The Giving of the Torah","body_en":"<p>In addition to the fact that at the revelation at Mount Sinai God revealed Himself to all of Israel, the main content of the revelation also dealt with faith, for the essence of the Ten Commandments is the first two commandments, which deal with faith, and these all of Israel heard in the most explicit manner from the mouth of the Almighty (below 16:10, note 7). From these two commandments we learn that the content of the faith of Israel is based on the revelation in which God revealed Himself to us when He took us out of Egypt and gave us the Torah and the mitzvot, and that we must base our faith only on this revelation, without mixing in any foreign idolatrous conception, as it is stated (Shemot 20:2-5): \"I am the Lord your God, who brought you out of the land of Egypt, out of the house of bondage. You shall have no other gods before Me. You shall not make for yourself a carved image or any likeness… You shall not bow down to them nor serve them…\" All the rest of the words of the Torah and its mitzvot are also branches of the first two commandments, as the Sages said (Tikkunei Zohar 22) that the root of all the positive commandments is in the commandment of \"I am,\" and the root of all the negative commandments is in the commandment of \"You shall have no.\"</p><p>It turns out, then, that the first revelations in which God revealed Himself to the Patriarchs concerned the general vision of the people of Israel to bring blessing to the world. But the precise guidance as to how to realize the vision God gave to Israel in the Torah that we received at the revelation at Mount Sinai.</p><p>At the opening of the Kuzari, Rabbi Yehuda Halevi tells of a king to whom an angel appeared in a dream and said to him: Your intention is pleasing in the eyes of God, but your deeds are not pleasing, for you do not know how to draw close to Him. The king then began to examine the various religions, and it became clear to him that in no religion had its people received a clear Divine revelation that guided their actions, except for the Jews, to whom God revealed Himself and gave His Torah. In this, Rabbi Yehuda Halevi explained that every person who has the breath of life in his nostrils wants his actions to be directed, precise and fitting in the eyes of God, and Israel merited that God chose them and gave them His Torah and His mitzvot, through which they can cleave to Him and bring goodness and blessing to themselves and to the world, as it is stated (Devarim 6:24): \"And the Lord commanded us to do all these statutes, to fear the Lord our God, for our good always, that He might keep us alive, as it is this day.\" Rabbi Yehuda Halevi compared Israel among the nations to the heart among the organs (Kuzari 2:36), for Israel serve as priests and ministers for all the nations, through whom life and blessing come to all the inhabitants of the world.</p>","notes_en":[]},{"slug":"20-01-10","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/20-01-10/","title_he":"י – אמונת הייחוד הישראלית","title_en":"The Israelite Faith in God's Unity","body_en":"<p>The essence of the Divine revelation is that the Lord, Creator of the world, is the one and only source of the entire universe and of the dimension of time, and that Israel can cleave to Him with love by walking in His ways, as it is stated (Devarim 6:4-5): \"Hear, O Israel, the Lord is our God, the Lord is One. And you shall love the Lord your God with all your heart, and with all your soul, and with all your might.\"</p><p>There are two aspects to \"faith in God's unity\" (emunat ha-yiḥud). The first: faith in God's unity negates every other power, and this is the prohibition of idolatry. That is, He is one, single and unique: one, with no second to Him; single, with none who can resemble Him; unique, not composed of different aspects. Consequently He has no image and cannot be defined by any attribute or definition (Rambam, Hilkhot Yesodei Ha-Torah 1:1-9).</p><p>The second aspect is positive, and it is the principal one for us. That is to say, faith in God's unity not only nullifies all the idols, but also teaches that all the powers and their qualities are Divine revelations. Therefore the name \"Elohim\" is a plural name, for it includes many powers whose source is one. This is the meaning of \"the Lord is God (Elohim)\": that is, the Lord, who is above all definition and is not grasped, is the one and only source of all the powers. This is unlike the conception of the idol worshippers, who separated all the powers from the source, and likewise separated them from one another, and related to each one of them as a separate idol. This is what is stated (Devarim 4:35): \"You were shown, so that you might know that the Lord is God; there is none else besides Him.\" And it is stated (ibid. 4:39): \"Know therefore this day, and take it to your heart, that the Lord is God in the heavens above and on the earth below; there is none else.\" And this is the meaning of the verse: \"Hear, O Israel, the Lord is our God, the Lord is One\" (Devarim 6:4). Hear, O Israel: the Lord is Elohim, that is, He is revealed through all the powers in the world, and all these revelations are one.<sup>5</sup></p><p>Around faith in God's unity all the inhabitants of the world are destined to unite, as Rashi explained (Devarim 6:4): \"Hear, O Israel, the Lord is our God – the Lord, who is our God now and not the God of the nations,\" will, through the work of the people of Israel, be One for all the nations, as it is stated (Tzefanya 3:9): \"For then I will turn to the peoples a pure language, that they may all call upon the name of the Lord, to serve Him with one accord.\"</p><p>Since, according to faith in God's unity, a Divine illumination is revealed in all created beings, faith in God's unity brings about a great love for all created beings, as our master Rav Kook wrote: \"One upon whom the light of faith is revealed in its purity loves all creatures, without any exception at all, and his whole concern is their elevation and repair\" (Middot Ha-Re'aya, Emuna 4). \"Faith and love are always joined to one another… A person is not suited to any spiritual power in the world with such great perfection as he is suited to faith and to love… for they are the very essence of life in this world and in the World to Come\" (Orot, Orot Ha-teḥiya 17).</p>","notes_en":[{"n":5,"en":"Some emphasized the negating aspect of faith in God's unity, and explained that all the attributes stated in the Torah in relation to God, such as wise and mighty, are not to be understood literally, since no definition can apply to God. Therefore, when we say that He is \"wise,\" the intention is not to define Him as \"wise,\" but to negate from Him the deficiency found in one who is not wise, and likewise with all the attributes. So wrote Ḥovot Ha-levavot, Sha'ar Ha-yiḥud 10; Rambam, Moreh Nevukhim 1:58; Ha-ikkarim 2:22-25; and others. However, the principal meaning of faith in God's unity is that all the powers are a Divine revelation. According to this, all the attributes are attributes of the Divine revelation and not of His essence, and therefore they are essential attributes of what is revealed to us. Rabbi Ḥasdai Crescas wrote along these lines (Or Hashem, Ma'amar 1, 3:3). So too the Kabbalists explained (Pardes Rimonim, Sha'ar 4, Atzmut Ve-kelim, ch. 10; Shomer Emunim, second debate, 51-55; Nefesh Ha-ḥayim 2:2-3). The description most fitting for Him, may He be blessed, is \"Ein Sof\" (the Infinite), as is found in the language of the Kabbalists. In the world it is customary to relate to \"infinite\" as \"not finite,\" such as an infinite number, that is, a number to which one can always add. But \"Ein Sof\" is above and beyond all concepts, such as absolute, eternal, all-inclusive, which are but a small spark of Him. As our master Rav Kook wrote (Orot, Zer'onim, \"Yissurim Memarkim\"): \"The essence of faith is in the greatness of the perfection of Ein Sof. For whatever enters the heart is a spark utterly negligible relative to what ought to be conceived, and what ought to be conceived does not even rise to the category of negligibility relative to what He truly is.\""}]},{"slug":"20-01-11","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/20-01-11/","title_he":"יא – המלחמה בעבודה זרה","title_en":"The War against Idolatry","body_en":"<p>In contrast to faith in God's unity, which is revealed in unity in all realms of life, the idolatrous conception creates separation and disconnection between the various values. The deepest separation is between heaven and earth. Heaven expresses absolute truth and supreme morality, and earth expresses the needs of life and bodily pleasures, and relative truth and morality with their limitations and deficiencies. Idolatry requires a person to choose between heaven and earth. By contrast, according to the Israelite faith in God's unity, heaven and earth are connected to each other and fructify each other, because the Lord is One; He is the Creator of heaven and earth and He gives life to all. Therefore the Divine idea must appear from the highest heavens to the depths of the earth, and elevate all realms of life: intellect and emotion, the life of the spirit and the life of action; agriculture, industry, commerce, science and art; the life of the individual, the family, society and the nation.</p><p>In order to reveal the Divine values in the totality of life, there must be an entire people whose common destiny, for all its tribes, is faith, that is, the revelation of the word of God and His blessing in all realms of life, until the repair of the world under the kingship of the Almighty. Therefore the Torah was given to the entire people of Israel and not to the righteous alone, and likewise the most important prophetic revelations were for the entire people of Israel. And such is the wording of the blessing over the Torah: \"Who has chosen us from all the peoples and given us His Torah.\" Therefore the verse that expresses faith in God's unity more than any other was said to the entire people of Israel (Devarim 6:4): \"Hear, O Israel, the Lord is our God, the Lord is One,\" for only the entire people of Israel can express complete faith in God.</p><p>Another fundamental difference between the faith of Israel and idolatry is that according to the idolatrous conception, since the higher powers that govern the world are limited, all wills and thoughts are consequently limited as well, and man becomes enslaved to them. This conception prevents a person from believing that it is possible to change the world for the better without end. By contrast, the faith of Israel is in God, who is above and beyond all comprehension, and to whose light and goodness there is no end. And although we are limited, and therefore will never be able to absorb the fullness of His light and goodness, since we are bound to Him through faith, we can always rise higher and absorb more of His light, and we can always add more goodness and blessing to the world. Thereby the faith of Israel reveals the image of God in man, makes him a free person, and arouses him to expand the boundaries of his consciousness without end. Already at the time Israel appeared in the world, this great faith, which brings freedom to the human spirit, began to be revealed, and this is the meaning of the Exodus from Egypt; therefore the festival of Pesaḥ is called the festival of freedom (see Peninei Halakha, Pesaḥ 1:2). When all of Israel return to their Land and reveal faith in all realms of life, we will rise to a higher level of faith and freedom, and redemption will come to the whole world.</p>","notes_en":[]},{"slug":"20-01-12","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/20-01-12/","title_he":"יב – האמונה הטבעית וסיבת הכפירה","title_en":"Natural Faith and the Cause of Heresy","body_en":"<p>Faith in God is a basic assumption in the tradition of Israel, for man by his nature believes (above, section 1); therefore the prophets and the Sages did not need to prove it, but only explained its meaning and its concepts. For the more faith is explained and revealed in all realms of life, the more its awareness will grow stronger in the intellect, in the emotions and in life as a whole. Conversely, if a person explains faith in a mistaken way, for example by describing God in a corporeal and limited manner, questions will arise in his mind, such as: How is it possible that God, who is perfect, created a world in which there are deficiencies? And how is it possible that God, who is good, would create a world in which there is evil? The more such questions grow stronger in his consciousness, the more doubts in faith will be aroused in him, which may even cause him to deny what he had defined as faith. And when they grow stronger still, they may at times even cause him to lose his trust that there is any correct faith at all, just as a person who has been deceived many times by many people may lose his trust in people in general, to the point of thinking that everyone is a cheat. Usually, however, after time passes, a person's nature prevails and he returns to believe, except for people who have been so disappointed with faith that they have decided to stubbornly deny it even when their nature is drawn toward it.</p><p>In recent generations, through the development of the natural sciences and the sciences of man and society, human consciousness has developed and expanded greatly. In the process, some of the explanations of faith that were accepted in the past no longer suffice in our day, and therefore more people have doubts in matters of faith. But however much a person succeeds in improving the conditions of his life, without faith he will not be able to give them meaning, and without meaning that stems from faith and values, he will lose the taste of life, go astray after his evil inclinations, and destroy and lay waste. The more we continue to delve into the Torah that God gave us and into all the ways it has given us for revealing faith in life, the more we will merit to give true and deeper meaning to human life and to all the great developments it has developed, and we will merit to continue acting together with God to advance the world toward its redemption. We will continue to learn about all of this in the concluding chapter of the book, chapter 30.</p>","notes_en":[]},{"slug":"20-01-13","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/20-01-13/","title_he":"יג – משמעותה של האמונה לכלל החיים","title_en":"The Meaning of Faith for All of Life","body_en":"<p>Faith is the foundation and essence of life, and therefore it is the root of all the mitzvot in the Torah, as the Sages said (Makkot 24a) that all 613 mitzvot are included in one mitzva, the mitzva of faith, as it is stated (Ḥavakuk 2:4): \"And the righteous shall live by his faith.\" In greater detail, the first two of the Ten Commandments, \"I am the Lord your God\" and \"You shall have no other gods before Me\" (Shemot 20:2-3), deal with faith. The Sages said that these two mitzvot are the foundation of the entire Torah, for the root of all 248 positive commandments in the Torah is in the mitzva to believe in God, and the root of all 365 negative commandments in the Torah is in the mitzva not to worship idols (Tikkunei Zohar 64a).</p><p>Since faith is the foundation of life, the more deeply we delve into it and refine it of every trace of idolatry and corporealization, the more purely we will be able to cleave to the Lord our God, and this cleaving will elevate us and inspire us to walk in the ways of uprightness and goodness. Therefore let us explain a little of the meaning of faith in God:</p><p>From the fact that God created man in His image, in the image of God, that is, with the power of choice and creativity to act in the world and change it, we learn that man's role is to choose the good, to walk in the ways of God, and to develop creation and guard it, as it is stated (Bereshit 2:15): \"to work it and to guard it.\"</p><p>From the fact that God is \"Ein Sof\" (Infinite) flows the infinite aspiration of man, who was created in the image of God, to rise higher, to perfect himself, and to improve his life in all realms and refine it forever.</p><p>From the fact that God is One, we learn that all the separate created beings are united at their foundation, for one Creator created them; therefore it is fitting for human beings to live in love with one another, and it is fitting for man to reveal the harmony in creation, so that all created beings assist and complement one another.</p><p>From the fact that God, Creator of all, has no definition whatsoever, we learn that He is revealed in all defined created beings without exception, in the heavens above and on the earth below. Consequently everything has value, and a special Divine spark gives it life, and man, who was created in the image of God, must reveal it in its full splendor.</p><p>Through this the meaning of the 613 mitzvot is understood, for they encompass all realms of life, as the Sages said (Makkot 23b) that the 248 positive commandments correspond to the 248 limbs that a person has, and the 365 negative commandments correspond to the 365 sinews that a person has, and to the 365 days of the year.</p><p>From the fact that God created everything, including what we perceive as darkness and evil, it is understood that all troubles and afflictions are for the good.</p><p>From the fact that God created time, we learn that the Divine element is revealed in all times, each time according to its special character.</p>","notes_en":[]},{"slug":"20-01-14","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/20-01-14/","title_he":"יד – מידת הענווה ואמונת ישראל","title_en":"The Quality of Humility and the Faith of Israel","body_en":"<p>The quality of humility is essential for absorbing faith, because it enables a person to focus on what is within his ability to understand, without trying to ascend beyond it. For as stated, about God Himself we cannot think or speak, because He is above all comprehension. By contrast, about what is revealed to us we can think and speak. This principle is alluded to in the Torah, which does not begin with the letter alef, that is, it does not begin with a description of the Creator Himself, but rather begins with the letter bet, that is, with creation, as it is stated (Bereshit 1:1): \"<b>B</b>ereshit (In the beginning) God created the heavens and the earth.\" Of all the faiths and religions, only Israel refrains from speaking about God Himself. And this does not weaken their faith; on the contrary, it strengthens it. It is like a person who tries to gaze at the sun, who will end up damaging his eyesight and will not be able to see anything properly. So too, one who errs and tries to think and speak about God Himself will not only fail to understand God, but will also fail to understand the direction and purpose of creation. But if he focuses on what is revealed to us, he will be able to cleave to God with all his heart, soul and might (see below 13:2, on the prohibition of \"you shall not stray\").</p><p>Therefore God praises Israel for their humility, as it is stated (Devarim 7:7): \"Not because you are more numerous than all the peoples did the Lord desire you and choose you, for you are the fewest of all the peoples.\" The Sages explained (Ḥullin 89a): \"The Holy One, blessed be He, said to Israel: I desire you, for even when I bestow greatness upon you, you diminish yourselves before Me. I gave greatness to Avraham, and he said before Me: 'I am but dust and ashes' (Bereshit 18:27). To Moshe and Aharon, and they said: 'And what are we?' (Shemot 16:7). To David, and he said: 'But I am a worm and not a man' (Tehillim 22:7). But the idol worshippers are not so. I gave greatness to Nimrod, and he said: 'Come, let us build ourselves a city and a tower whose top is in the heavens' (Bereshit 11:4). To Pharaoh, and he said: 'Who is the Lord that I should heed His voice?!' (Shemot 5:2). To Sanḥeriv, and he said: 'Who among all the gods of the lands has saved their land from my hand, that the Lord should save Jerusalem from my hand?!' (II Melakhim 18:35). To Nevukhadnetzar, and he said: 'I will ascend above the heights of the clouds; I will be like the Most High' (Yeshayahu 14:14).\" To Ḥiram king of Tyre, and he said: \"I am a god! I sit in the seat of God, in the heart of the seas!\" (Yeḥezkel 28:2).</p><p>This quality of humility is directed toward God, and therefore there is no weakness or smallness in it; on the contrary, out of the recognition of what is impossible to understand, one knows that what can be understood can be understood well, namely, what God revealed to His creatures through creation, and what He revealed to Israel through the Torah and prophecy. In continuation of this, out of this humility of not dealing with what is beyond comprehension, confidence grows in all the interpretations and clarifications of the Sages of the Oral Torah, all of whose words are true. For God gave the Torah to the Sages of Israel, so that through their study they would bring into actuality the Divine will hidden in the Torah and in Israel. Out of this confidence, Israel are prepared to give their lives for their faith, and they act with strength and self-sacrifice to reveal the word of God and His blessing to Israel and to the world.</p><p>Precisely because the faith of Israel restrains itself from dealing with God, who is above and beyond all comprehension, it deals with the full extent of the Divine revelation in the world and in man, and thereby it encompasses the whole of a person's life. And this is its principal uniqueness, for in terms of intense feelings of faith, members of the various nations can also reach high peaks like Israel. Likewise, in philosophical clarification, which deals with the Divine source and strives to cleanse it of all corporealization (below, section 18), the sages of the nations of the world can also reach high achievements like Israel. But only in Israel is faith revealed in all realms of life, in the 613 mitzvot as guided by the Torah, and from this comes its strength and its blessing, which extends to the whole world (Rav Kook, the essay \"Da'at Elohim,\" pp. 135-136).</p>","notes_en":[]},{"slug":"20-01-15","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/20-01-15/","title_he":"טו – ידיעה ובחירה","title_en":"Foreknowledge and Free Choice","body_en":"<p>The question of foreknowledge and free choice is a clear example of the need for the quality of humility in order to absorb faith in its purity. Seemingly there is a contradiction between Divine knowledge and free choice. On the one hand, it is among the foundations of faith that God granted man free choice, and it is a good gift, through which the good that a person has comes to him by merit of his choice and not as an act of grace. There is no greater and deeper good than this, for it is akin to the Divine good (Derekh Hashem 1:2-3). Not only that, but thereby God granted man the privilege of being His partner in guarding the world and perfecting it, as it is stated (Bereshit 2:15): \"And the Lord God took the man and placed him in the Garden of Eden, to work it and to guard it.\" Since man has free choice, he bears responsibility for his actions: if he is righteous, he will merit reward, and if he is wicked, he will be punished. On the other hand, since God is all-powerful, it follows that He knows everything that will happen, including who will be righteous and who wicked; if so, how is it possible that man has free choice, when all the deeds he will do are already known? And if there is no free choice, there is no justice in God rewarding the righteous and punishing the wicked.</p><p>Because of this question, some tended to limit Divine knowledge, and some tended to limit free choice, and as a result tended toward an idolatrous conception or toward heresy. But according to the faith of Israel, which rests upon the trait of humility, the answer to this question is beyond our comprehension. For God's knowledge differs essentially from the knowledge familiar to us, as it is stated (Yeshayahu 55:8-9): \"For My thoughts are not your thoughts, nor are your ways My ways, says the Lord. For as the heavens are higher than the earth, so are My ways higher than your ways, and My thoughts than your thoughts.\" Among human beings, when it is known absolutely that a certain thing will happen, there is no longer any choice regarding it. But Divine knowledge is entirely different, and therefore it does not prevent free choice. For example, human knowledge knows things that are outside the person and outside his knowledge, whereas Divine knowledge is knowledge of things from within, for He is the Knower, the Known and the Knowledge (Rambam, Hilkhot Teshuva 5:5; Hilkhot Yesodei Ha-Torah 2:10; see below, section 20).</p><p>Thus, on the one hand, faith that God knows everything grants confidence that everything is for the good, and therefore even in the most difficult situations one must not lose faith. On the other hand, faith that God granted man free choice, and that it is in his hands to influence the whole world for good or for bad, places upon him the full responsibility to refrain from evil deeds, and arouses him to devote his entire being to adding goodness and blessing to the world, in order to advance the world toward its redemption.</p><p>Indeed, there were some among the Sages of Israel who tried to make this more comprehensible by adding explanations to this question, but all agree on the principle that God's knowledge itself is impossible to understand, and that man has free choice to change the world for good or for bad.<sup>6</sup></p>","notes_en":[{"n":6,"en":"Many explained that although God's knowledge of the future is absolute, it does not compel a person to do what he is expected to do in an absolute manner (Rav Saadia Gaon, Emunot Ve-de'ot 4:4; Kuzari 5:19-20; Ra'avad, Hilkhot Teshuva 5:5). The Kabbalists explained that knowledge belongs to one world and choice to another world (Pardes Rimonim, Sha'ar Atzmut Ve-kelim 9; Shelah, Introduction, Beit Ha-beḥira). But although their words are true, they do not resolve the question of how it is possible that God knows absolutely what exactly a person will do and nevertheless he has free choice. Therefore, after all, the accepted explanation is that the answer to this question is beyond our comprehension, as the Rambam wrote in his golden words in Hilkhot Teshuva 5:5. And so wrote the Or Same'aḥ there. And so wrote Sefer Ha-ikkarim 4:1-3; Abarbanel, Bereshit 18:20. Indeed, there were some among the Sages of Israel who tended to limit choice, such as Rabbi Ḥasdai Crescas (Or Hashem 2:5), who explained that there is indeed no choice regarding the deeds but only regarding the intention that accompanies them. Yet the question still remains how there is choice regarding the intention, given that it is known to God in advance. On the other side, some limited Divine knowledge, as if God limited His knowledge in order to grant man free choice. The Ralbag inclined toward this (the 16th lesson [to'elet] on Parashat Vayera). Yet this answer too does not resolve the question, since it follows from it that God is not all-powerful, and if He is not all-powerful, what is it that preceded Him and is above Him? Therefore the true answer, which is accepted in the tradition of Israel, is that comprehension of Divine knowledge is beyond our grasp, and therefore we cannot speak about it, as the Rambam said. And it seems reasonable that all agree on this principle, that God's knowledge is above our comprehension, and all their attempts to interpret it were meant to bring it closer to our understanding, but they did not claim that their explanations resolving the question of foreknowledge and free choice are the absolute truth (see Orot Ha-kodesh, vol. 2, p. 549). In any event, they too, like all the Sages of Israel, did not budge from the fundamental principle of the faith of Israel, that God knows everything and man bears full responsibility for his choices.<br>On this matter it is worth citing the explanation of my colleague Rav Ze'ev Sultanovitch regarding the concept of \"Ein Sof,\" that it includes all possibilities, and from the perspective of Ein Sof there is no difference between something that is possible and something that already exists in reality, for every idea is also considered reality. This is not so with any finite creature, which is limited, and for which every choice is only one possibility out of the other possibilities. The concepts of time and space as well, within which man's free choice takes place and which limit choice to one choice out of countless choices, do not apply to Ein Sof. Consequently, the question about the supposed contradiction between foreknowledge and free choice is nullified at its root."}]},{"slug":"20-01-16","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/20-01-16/","title_he":"טז – שכר ועונש בעולם הזה ובעולם הבא","title_en":"Reward and Punishment in This World and in the World to Come","body_en":"<p>Divine providence, too, we cannot fully understand, and toward it too we are required to have humility. Let us briefly mention here the matter of reward and punishment (as explained in Peninei Halakha, Yamim Nora'im, chapter 1): since man has free choice, he bears responsibility for his actions, and it is just that he receive good reward for his good deeds and punishment for his bad deeds. And such is also the nature of creation: if he chooses the good, he will draw close to God, the Source of life, and will merit abundant life, and will add blessing to himself and to the whole world. And if he chooses evil, he will distance himself from God and will cause troubles and death to himself and to the whole world. And God wants us to choose the good, as it is stated (Devarim 30:19): \"I call heaven and earth to witness against you this day: I have set before you life and death, the blessing and the curse; therefore choose life, that you may live, you and your descendants.\"</p><p>In order for man to have free choice, so that the good he merits will truly belong to him, there must necessarily be a time in which human life is conducted according to the laws of nature; for if every person who chose the good immediately received reward, and everyone who chose evil were immediately punished, there would be no free choice, and man would not merit to reveal the image of God within him. Therefore God established that there would be a period in which man chooses, and afterward would come the stage in which reward and punishment are given to him (see Peninei Halakha, Yamim Nora'im 1:1-2, 6-7). Indeed, already in this world the righteous merit that the reward is revealed in the depths of their souls, for they merit closeness to God, and their lives are filled with value and meaning, and this is more precious to them than anything (ibid. 1:5-6). But outwardly, Divine governance is hidden, and there are righteous people who suffer afflictions and wicked people who prosper, and it appears as though God has abandoned the earth.</p><p>Originally, reward and punishment were supposed to appear in this world, but since the sin of Adam a separation was created between the worlds, between this world and the World to Come, between the body and the soul. Therefore the Divine abundance cannot be revealed in its fullness in this world, and the body cannot receive the fullness of life from the soul, and therefore it ultimately dies. In continuation of this, reward and punishment cannot arrive in their fullness in this physical world; rather, only a small part of the reward and punishment takes place in this world. A larger part takes place after a person's death, in the world of souls, in Gan Eden and in Gehinnom. And the completion of the reward will be at the time of the resurrection of the dead, when the world will return to its rectified state, and the worlds will be reunited, and the soul and the body will be reunited. There are many explanations and discussions in the words of the Sages about reward and punishment in the world of souls and in the World to Come, and the rule is that God desires the good of His creatures, and therefore the reward stored up for the righteous is the best reward, and the punishment is the most beneficial for the repair of man and the world (see ibid. 1:5-7).</p><p>God chose His people Israel in order to redeem the world through them from its corruption. Therefore, already in the life of the people of Israel in this world, the unity between soul and body is revealed, and already in this world reward and punishment are revealed in the Jewish people as a whole. When Israel walk in the ways of God, they merit spiritual and material blessing alike, and when they sin, the Shekhina departs from them and they are punished with afflictions and exile; until the redemption comes and Israel return to their Land and walk in the ways of God, \"and the Lord shall be King over all the earth,\" \"on that day living waters shall go out from Jerusalem,\" to bring blessing to the whole world (Zekharya 14:8-9). And the blessing will extend to all creatures: \"And the wolf shall dwell with the lamb, and the leopard shall lie down with the kid… for the earth shall be full of the knowledge of the Lord, as the waters cover the sea\" (Yeshayahu 11:6-9).</p>","notes_en":[]},{"slug":"20-01-17","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/20-01-17/","title_he":"יז – שאלת הרע בעולם","title_en":"The Question of Evil in the World","body_en":"<p>One of the questions that pierce to the depths of the soul: if God desires the good of His creatures, how does He consent to there being so much suffering in the world? How does He allow the wicked to be so cruel to others? The answer, at once simple and profound, is that God created the world and all that is in it in order to do good, and evil and afflictions are also meant to do good, but man's view is too narrow and limited to see the big picture, in this world and in the World to Come. Therefore, in the eyes of flesh and blood, evil appears terrible and dreadful, but when one understands that everything God does is for the good, one understands the importance of evil. This is also the answer that God gave to Iyov: that his mind was too small to recognize the complexity of creation and to see how everything leads to the good.</p><p>As with the questions relating to God Himself, we find once again the necessity of wrapping ourselves in humility and knowing that there are things we are incapable of understanding, including that we are incapable of seeing the proper place of evil in the big picture. Out of this we will be able to strengthen ourselves in the faith that everything God does, He does for the good, and to begin to decipher the meaning of evil, and to understand how evil can advance us; and by this power we will act as much as we can to add goodness and blessing to the world.</p><p>Let us explain a little of the good that grows through evil. First, through the existence of evil man has free choice, and thereby the image of God within him is revealed, for he chooses and creates his world, and so the good belongs to him. And there is no greater good than this, for it is akin to the Divine good (Ramḥal, Derekh Hashem 1:2-3). That is to say, free choice is so precious that it is worth paying for it the price that there will be those who choose evil, with all the suffering this entails.</p><p>In addition to evil enabling free choice, through evil the good improves and is elevated. For all things in the world are limited, and this includes the revealed good, which is also limited. In order for the good to be able to continue developing without limit, and thereby express the Divine light within it, it must be criticized. And this is the role of evil: to point out the deficiencies in the good and to arouse continued elevation and repair. Indeed, it often happens that when a certain event occurred, people thought it was the worst thing for them, and in the end it became the most important event in their lives, because thanks to it they grew more than from anything else.<sup>7</sup></p><p>People of faith further add that, in truth, when one looks at the world with a good eye, one finds that the good outweighs the evil, for a person lives many years in health, and is sick for only a few of his days. Even when one organ of his body is sick, very many organs are healthy. Even when there is a person who harms him, he can rejoice in the many people who do not harm him. By thanking God for all the good in the world in the passages of praise in prayer and in the blessings over enjoyments, one becomes accustomed to seeing all the good in the world and rejoicing in it. And so the Rambam wrote (Moreh Nevukhim 3:12), that most of the world is good. He added that the evil in the world is divided into three parts: 1) a small part of it stems from natural disasters; 2) much more is caused by what a person inflicts on others; 3) and much more still by what a person inflicts on himself.<sup>8</sup></p>","notes_en":[{"n":7,"en":"Some still ask: why would God not create a world in which a person could merit all the virtues of choice and growth without evil? Some explain that God created the world according to the rules of wisdom, and according to them it is necessary that in a created and limited world there will be evil (Moreh Nevukhim 3:12; Derekh Hashem, part 1, 2:1-2; Olat Re'aya, vol. 1, p. 165). After all, the answer, at once simple and profound, which is based on the humility required for faith, is that we do not understand everything, but we believe that everything that comes from God is for the good, and only in our eyes, for the time being, is it perceived as evil. Sometimes a person merits to see this during his life in this world, and sometimes he will merit to see it only in the World of Truth."},{"n":8,"en":"One may mention here the Buddhist approach to evil and suffering. According to it, if a person stops craving and turns his gaze to life itself, which is not dependent on anything other than life itself, he will not experience sorrow and suffering, and will be able to enjoy a deep and supreme tranquility. One can learn from their approach that the feeling of suffering depends to a great extent on our consciousness. According to Buddhism, it is proper to rid oneself of the feeling of suffering; according to Judaism, it is proper to experience it, since it serves as an engine for growth and progress. But when a person suffers too much, he can learn useful techniques from the Buddhist approach in order to acquire indifference to suffering, and through this he will strengthen himself in the faith in God that everything is for the good, until he once again finds the path of growth and progress (see further below 8:6-7)."}]},{"slug":"20-01-18","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/20-01-18/","title_he":"יח – הוכחות שכליות","title_en":"Rational Proofs","body_en":"<p>Some of the great Sages of Israel, beginning in the period of the Ge'onim and the Rishonim, engaged in strengthening faith in God with the help of rational proofs. Let us briefly summarize three principal arguments:</p><p>The first: causality, the cosmological argument. If we accept the principle of creation, regardless of the theory according to which it occurred, including the theories of the Big Bang (for the existence of the universe) and evolution (for the existence of the diverse life in the world), then we must assume that there is a cause for the process of creation having begun, and a cause for the cause, until we are compelled to reach the conclusion that there is an infinite First Cause, by whose power everything operates. Even if we accept the view that the world is eternal and was not created, there must be a cause for its existence, for it cannot rest upon itself, since it is itself limited; therefore we are compelled to conclude that it rests upon an infinite cause, which is God.</p><p>The second: the principle of purpose, the teleological argument. Since we see such a complex and ordered world, there is no escaping the assumption that there is One who ordered it for a particular purpose. That is to say, the previous argument related to existence itself, which requires a cause, whereas this argument relates to the character of existence, which is wisely ordered toward some goal.</p><p>The third: human consciousness, the ontological argument (the argument from being). According to this argument, the existence of God is proven from the fact that it is present in human consciousness, for a person cannot think of something that does not exist and cannot exist, but only of something that exists in reality. And if the concept of God exists in human consciousness, then He exists in reality.</p><p>As stated, beginning in the period of the Ge'onim and the Rishonim, some of the great figures of Israel engaged in philosophical proofs for faith; but there were also many sages of their generation who explained faith logically but not with philosophical tools. Apparently the engagement in philosophical proofs appeared following the rise of philosophical discussion among the sages of the nations of the world, and since faith must appear in all modes of consciousness, the need arose to express it with philosophical tools as well. Likewise, as a result of the lowly state of Israel in exile, doubts arose among Jews about the faith of Israel, which seemed to have failed; at the same time, fierce attacks on the faith of Israel arose from the adherents of the various religions, and there was a need to defend it with philosophical tools as well.</p><p>After all, it is proper for every student of Torah to be familiar with these proofs; but most of the Sages of Israel did not rest their faith on the philosophical proofs, but rather on the precise and faithful tradition passed down from generation to generation. Rational clarification was nonetheless important in their eyes, because through it faith is cleansed of every dross of corporealization.<sup>9</sup></p>","notes_en":[{"n":9,"en":"On the causal argument, the following wrote: Rav Saadia Gaon, Emunot Ve-de'ot 1; Ḥovot Ha-levavot, Sha'ar Ha-yiḥud 6; Moreh Nevukhim 2:2. On the teleological argument, the following wrote: Ḥovot Ha-levavot, ibid.; Moreh Nevukhim 2:22. On human consciousness, later philosophers such as Descartes wrote. Some wrote that it is a choice mitzva (mitzva min ha-muvḥar) to study these arguments: Moreh Nevukhim 1:36; 1:71; Sefer Ha-ḥinukh 25. And Rabbeinu Baḥya (Ḥovot Ha-levavot, Sha'ar Ha-yiḥud 3) wrote that whoever is able to understand them is even obligated to study them. Likewise the Shelah wrote in Asara Ma'amarot, Ma'amar 1, that there is an obligation to study well all the words of Rabbeinu Baḥya in Sha'ar Ha-yiḥud. And the Gra said that instead of Sha'ar Ha-yiḥud one should study the Kuzari (Tosefet Ma'aseh Rav), and these words of his were often on the lips of my teacher and master, Rav Tzvi Yehuda Kook. The Kuzari bases faith on the precise tradition, and explains it logically, thereby removing the philosophical difficulties from it."}]},{"slug":"20-01-19","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/20-01-19/","title_he":"יט – י\"ג העיקרים לרמב\"ם ודעת החולקים","title_en":"The Rambam's Thirteen Principles and the Opinion of Those Who Disagree","body_en":"<p>The Rambam summarized the foundations of the faith of Israel in thirteen principles (Introduction to Perek Ḥelek). Briefly, they are: 1) the existence of God; 2) the unity of God; 3) that He has no body; 4) that He is eternal; 5) that only He is fit to be worshipped; 6) prophecy; 7) the prophecy of Moshe Rabbeinu; 8) Torah from Heaven; 9) the eternity of the Torah; 10) providence;<br>11) reward and punishment; 12) the Messiah; 13) the resurrection of the dead.</p><p>Some disagree with the Rambam on the enumeration of the principles, foremost among them Rabbi Yosef Albo, who even arranged his book, Sefer Ha-ikkarim, around this subject. In his opinion, only three fundamental principles should be counted, which include all of the Rambam's principles: the existence of God, Torah from Heaven, and providence.</p><p>In addition, it emerges from the words of many of the great figures of Israel that there are further principles among the foundations of the faith of Israel, such as the chosenness of the people of Israel and of the Land of Israel, as explained at length in the Kuzari of Rabbi Yehuda Halevi. Rav David Cohen, \"the Rav Ha-Nazir,\" inferred from the words of the Kuzari that there are five principles: the chosenness of Israel, the Land of Israel, Torah from Heaven, prophecy, and reward and punishment (Ha-Kuzari Ha-meforash 1:11).<sup>10</sup></p><p>Sefer Ha-ikkarim (1:3) further raised a difficulty against the Rambam: why did he not count free choice as one of the principles, given that the Rambam himself wrote about it: \"This principle is a great principle, and it is the pillar of the Torah and the commandment\" (Hilkhot Teshuva 5:3)? Some explain that this principle is included in the principle of reward and punishment, for it is impossible to give reward and punishment to one who has no choice. But if so, it would have been possible to include all thirteen principles under the three principles of Rabbi Yosef Albo, which, as stated, are: the existence of God, Torah, and providence, which includes reward and punishment.</p><p>One may explain that the Rambam brought principles connected to man's relationship to God, whereas choice is something God implanted in man. Likewise, the chosenness of Israel concerns man, and not man's relationship to God.</p><p>And some of the great figures of Israel did not agree to establish principles, because the entire Torah is a principle from the mouth of the Almighty, as the Sages said (Sanhedrin 99a) that whoever says that even one verse of the Torah is not from the mouth of the Almighty is considered as one who says that the entire Torah is not from Heaven, and he thereby despises the word of God and has no share in the World to Come (Abarbanel, Rosh Amana 23; Radbaz 1:344).</p><p>After all, many of the great figures of Israel accepted the thirteen principles established by the Rambam as a successful summary of the principles of faith. And in many siddurim the Rambam's thirteen principles were even printed in an abridged version, to help those who wish to review the foundations of faith. Therefore let us explain them briefly.</p>","notes_en":[{"n":10,"en":"Rabbeinu Ḥananel, who died about eighty years before the Rambam was born, wrote (Shemot 14:31) that faith is divided into four parts: faith in God, in the prophets, in the World to Come, and in the coming of the Messiah. Rabbi Ḥasdai Crescas, who was born about a hundred and forty years after the Rambam's death, wrote (Or Hashem, beginning of Ma'amar 2) that there are six principles: God's knowledge, His providence, His power, prophecy, choice, and purpose. His disciple, Rabbi Yosef Albo, counted three principles: the existence of God, Torah from Heaven, and God's providence. And so wrote Rabbi Abba Mari Ha-Yarḥi (a contemporary of the Rashba) in his book Minḥat Kena'ot (4), and the Tashbetz in his book Ohev Mishpat on Iyov (8)."}]},{"slug":"20-01-20","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/20-01-20/","title_he":"כ – חמשת העיקרים העוסקים במציאות ה'","title_en":"The Five Principles Dealing with the Existence of God","body_en":"<p><b>The first principle – God is the Creator of the world and sustains it</b>, as it is stated (Bereshit 1:1): \"In the beginning God created the heavens and the earth.\" From this one must conclude, as the Rambam wrote (Introduction to Perek Ḥelek): \"that there exists a Being who exists in the most perfect mode of existence,\" and that He created the world.</p><p>God's creation of the world is not like a carpenter who made a vessel, for after the carpenter has finished making the vessel, the vessel exists without him, whereas the Creator sustains His world at every moment, and were He to stop sustaining it for one moment, its existence would cease and be reduced to nothing. This is what is stated (Neḥemya 9:6): \"And You give life to them all,\" that is to say, You bring them all into being. But if the entire world were to cease to exist, nothing would change in God's existence. As the Sages said (Bereshit Rabba 68:9): \"He is the place of the world, but His world is not His place.\" Therefore God, Creator of the world, is perfect in every perfection, and His existence is above and beyond every existence in all the worlds and dimensions. And we cannot understand anything of His existence, since a limited intellect is incapable of understanding the unlimited. And all that we understand is in what is revealed to us and not in Him Himself. Likewise all the names by which God is called describe God's revelations to us, each name expressing a particular revelation.<sup>11</sup> From the first foundation the next four foundations necessarily follow.</p><p><b>The second principle – His unity</b>: God, Creator of the world, is One, as it is stated (Devarim 6:4): \"Hear, O Israel, the Lord is our God, the Lord is One.\" That is to say, although He is the source of all the many created beings, and the source of all the various Divine actions, He Himself is One. This is unlike the error of the idol worshippers, who, because they saw various forces in the world, concluded that there are many gods. God's unity is unique of its kind, and there is nothing like it in the world, because every created being of which it is said that it is one is composed of different parts. For example: one person is composed of different organs and different traits. Likewise one stone can be divided into separate parts. Whereas He, may He be blessed, is One in whom no division whatsoever applies.</p><p>God's knowledge, too, is His essence. That is to say, unlike man, who has an intellect and by means of it comprehends various things that are external to him, with God everything is one, for there are no things outside of Him; rather, by knowing Himself He knows everything. \"He is the Knower, and He is the Known, and He is the Knowledge itself – all is one. And this matter the mouth has no power to say, nor the ear to hear, nor the human heart to fully grasp… Therefore He does not recognize and know the creatures by virtue of the creatures, as we know them, but He knows them by virtue of Himself. Therefore, because He knows Himself, He knows everything, for everything depends on Him for its existence\" (Rambam, Hilkhot Yesodei Ha-Torah 2:10; and see Moreh Nevukhim 1:57-58).</p><p><b>The third principle – that He has no body</b>, as it is stated (Devarim 4:12-15): \"And the Lord spoke to you from the midst of the fire; you heard the sound of words, but you saw no form, only a voice… Therefore guard yourselves very well, for you saw no form on the day that the Lord spoke to you at Ḥorev from the midst of the fire.\" Even the speech that God spoke to us at Ḥorev, or to His prophets, is not like human speech by means of a mouth; rather, it is speech that God created so that it would be heard by us (Moreh Nevukhim 1:65). And all the verses that seemingly imply that God has a body are not to be taken literally, but were said in order to make things comprehensible, so that through them we will understand God's activity in the world, for \"the Torah speaks in the language of human beings.\"</p><p><b>The fourth principle – that God preceded everything and created the world.</b> That is to say, God is above and beyond time, and the future and the past are one for Him, for time itself is a creation that God created, and only after God created time is it possible to define past, present and future (Moreh Nevukhim 2:13).</p><p><b>The fifth principle – that only God is fit to be worshipped</b>, and not any other gods, as it is stated (Shemot 20:4 [?]): \"You shall have no other gods before Me.\" The error of the idol worshippers was that they held that although the Lord is the God of gods, nevertheless the great created beings also have independent power, and they can bestow good on whoever worships them. About this the Torah warned (Devarim 11:16): \"Take heed for yourselves, lest your heart be deceived, and you turn aside and serve other gods and bow down to them.\"</p>","notes_en":[{"n":11,"en":"The Rambam defined this foundation as follows: \"that there exists a Being who exists in the most perfect mode of existence, and He is the cause of the existence of all that exists, and in Him their existence is sustained.\" But since His perfection is not grasped by us, and all that we know about Him is in what has been revealed to us, I opened with what the Torah opened with: that God created the world. This principle is also explained in Hilkhot Yesodei Ha-Torah 1:1-6, and he wrote there (1:3): \"His truth is not like the truth of any one of them.\" And there (1:6): \"And knowledge of this matter is a positive commandment, as it is stated: 'I am the Lord your God.'\" And so he explained in Moreh Nevukhim 1:57; 61; 69.<br>All the holy names express God's revelations to us, and the Ineffable Name, which because of its great holiness may not be pronounced except by the kohanim in the Temple, and which is not written except in Torah scrolls, is Y-H-V-H. The meaning of this name is that God is eternal: He was, He is, and He will always be (SA OḤ 5:1); and also that He brings all created beings into being at every moment (Tanya, Sha'ar Ha-yiḥud Ve-ha-emuna 4; Ha'amek Davar, Shemot 6:3; Rav S. R. Hirsch, Bereshit 2:4). \"Brings into being\" – that is, gives life to and sustains."}]},{"slug":"20-01-21","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/20-01-21/","title_he":"כא – ארבעת העיקרים העוסקים בתורה","title_en":"The Four Principles Dealing with the Torah","body_en":"<p><b>The sixth principle – prophecy.</b> That is, that God reveals His will to chosen individuals among mankind. In order to be worthy of prophecy, a person must be wise, righteous, a doer of good deeds, and also mighty, not tempted after his inclinations or after the opinion of people. The subject of prophecy will be explained at length below in chapter 16.</p><p><b>The seventh principle – the prophecy of Moshe Rabbeinu.</b> \"That he is the father of all the prophets who preceded him and who came after him… and he is the chosen of God from all mankind, who attained of Him, may He be exalted, more than any person who has existed or will exist has attained or will attain… No veil remained before him that he did not tear, no bodily obstacle held him back, and no deficiency at all remained in him, neither small nor great…\" And it was he who received the Torah from Sinai. The subjects of the prophecy of Moshe and the revelation at Mount Sinai will be explained below in the chapter on prophecy (16:9-10).</p><p><b>The eighth principle – Torah from Heaven.</b> \"That is, that we believe that this entire Torah that is in our hands this day is the Torah that was given to Moshe, and that all of it is from the mouth of the Almighty.\" And we should know that all of it is holy, and that there is no difference between the verses dealing with the names of the nations, such as \"And the sons of Ḥam: Kush, and Mitzrayim, and Put, and Canaan\" (Bereshit 10:6), and the verse \"Hear, O Israel, the Lord is our God, the Lord is One\" (Devarim 6:4). \"All is from the mouth of the Almighty, and all is the Torah of the Lord, perfect, pure, holy, true.\" And one who says that the entire Torah is from the mouth of God except for one verse is counted among the heretics. \"Rather, in every letter of it there are wisdoms and wonders for one whom God has given understanding, and the ultimate end of its wisdom cannot be attained; its measure is longer than the earth and wider than the sea.\" Included in this foundation is faith in the tradition of the Sages, that \"its accepted interpretation is also from the mouth of the Almighty, and that the form of the sukka, the lulav, the shofar, the tzitzit, the tefillin and the like that we make today is the very form that God told Moshe, and he told us.\"</p><p><b>The ninth principle – that the Torah will never change.</b> \"That this Torah of Moshe will not be annulled, and no other Torah will come from God besides it, and nothing will be added to it or taken away from it, neither in the written text nor in the interpretation,\" as it is stated (Devarim 13:1): \"You shall not add to it, nor take away from it.\"</p>","notes_en":[]},{"slug":"20-01-22","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/20-01-22/","title_he":"כב – ארבעת העיקרים העוסקים בהשגחה ובשכר ועונש","title_en":"The Four Principles Dealing with Providence and with Reward and Punishment","body_en":"<p><b>The tenth principle – God knows all that is done in the world</b>, as it is stated (Yirmeyahu 32:19): \"Great in counsel and mighty in deed, whose eyes are open upon all the ways of the children of men, to give every man according to his ways and according to the fruit of his deeds.\"<sup>12</sup></p><p><b>The eleventh principle – reward and punishment.</b> \"That He, may He be exalted, pays good reward to one who fulfills the commandments of the Torah, and punishes one who transgresses its prohibitions.\" A person receives some of the reward and punishment in this world, but its main part is in the World to Come, where the reward in the World to Come is the great good that has no end, and the most severe punishment is karet, in which the soul is lost and will not merit to receive reward in the World to Come, as it is stated (Bemidbar 15:31): \"That soul shall surely be cut off (hikaret tikaret); its iniquity is upon it\": \"Hikaret – in this world; tikaret – in the World to Come\" (Sanhedrin 64b).</p><p><b>The twelfth principle – the days of the Messiah.</b> \"And that is to believe that he will come and will not be late. Though he tarry, wait for him.\" One should not set a time for it, nor try to interpret the verses in order to determine by them the time of the Messiah's coming. The Sages said (Sanhedrin 97b): \"May the bones of those who calculate the end-times rot.\" Included in this foundation is to believe that the King Messiah will be from the descendants of David and Shlomo.</p><p><b>The thirteenth principle – the resurrection of the dead.</b> According to the Rambam, the resurrection of the dead for the righteous is not the essence of the reward, but rather a miracle, which apparently is needed to repair what was not completed during life on earth; but the principal reward is in the World to Come, which is the eternal world of the spirit. However, according to most of the Sages of Israel, the essence of the World to Come is after the resurrection of the dead, in a perfect union of soul and body (see Peninei Halakha, Yamim Nora'im 1:5).</p>","notes_en":[{"n":12,"en":"<b>The discussion of \"individual providence\" (hashgaḥa pratit)</b>: Seemingly there is a dispute about the extent of God's providence over His creatures. According to the Rambam (Moreh Nevukhim 3:17), God watches over \"only the individuals of the human species… and the good and evil that come upon them stem from justice, as it is stated (Devarim 32:4): 'For all His ways are justice.'\" But regarding animals, plants and the like, \"I do not at all believe that this leaf fell because of providence over it, nor that this spider devoured this fly by virtue of a decree of God and His particular will at that moment… rather, all these, in my opinion, are by pure chance… What brought me to this belief is that I have not found it written in a book of prophecy that God has providence over a particular individual animal, but only over the individual human being.\" That is to say, providence over the animal, plant and inanimate realms is exercised over the species as a whole, but what happens to each individual member of that species happens according to the laws of nature and statistics that God created. In Moreh Nevukhim chapter 18 he added that even over human beings Divine providence is not equal, but rather the more a person rises, the greater God's providence over him. And such is the opinion of the Ralbag (Milḥamot Hashem, Ma'amar 4), the Meiri (Berakhot 33b; Megilla 25b), the Ḥinukh (169, 545), the Radak (I Melakhim 13:28; Tehillim 145:17), Sefer Ha-ikkarim (4:8; 10), and others. On the other hand, however, according to the great Ḥasidic masters, there is nothing that is not watched over by God individually, and even each and every blade of grass grows or withers by individual providence. So said the Ba'al Shem Tov, and so wrote Rabbi Menaḥem Mendel of Vitebsk (Pri Ha-aretz, Bo). And so we find in several Rishonim and in midrashim of Ḥazal. However, it seems that there is no deep dispute here, for all agree that there is no end to God's knowledge and ability. Moreover, \"leit atar panui minei,\" there is no place devoid of Him (Tikkunei Zohar 122b). And even the Rambam explained in the first and second principles that God gives life to everything at every moment and knows everything with a self-knowledge; consequently there is nothing in the world that is not watched over by Him. Rather, the entire discussion concerns our awareness. The Rambam explained that we are incapable of recognizing the Divine providence over each and every detail of creation other than man, and even with man, the higher the level he is on, the more it is possible to recognize the individual providence over him. This means that if a person who has not reached the proper level of understanding contemplates the individual providence over himself, he will live in false imaginings, and his inclination will lead him to think that he merited blessing by virtue of deeds that are not worthy of blessing, or that he was punished as a result of deeds that are not deserving of punishment, and the like. Whereas the Ba'al Shem Tov and those who followed him explained that one must see God's providence in everything, but they do not presume to understand every detail that happens; rather, since in truth there is a Divine revelation in every detail, it is possible to receive from it a general, unconscious impression, and at times even to attain something from a particular event that happened to an animal or a plant, and this, for them, was the meaning of knowing the speech of the birds and the trees. (Incidentally, the discussion about the Divine tzimtzum (contraction), whether it is to be taken literally, is likewise nothing but a discussion of what our consciousness attains, and not a discussion of the matter itself, which we are entirely unable to attain.)"}]},{"slug":"20-01-23","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/20-01-23/","title_he":"כג – האמונה בתודעה ברגש ובכלל החיים","title_en":"Faith in Consciousness, in Emotion and in All of Life","body_en":"<p>Since the faith of Israel is revealed in all realms of life, there is a mitzva to establish it in three circles that encompass human life: in consciousness, in emotion, and in all of life.</p><p><b>In consciousness</b>, through the study of faith and its meaning, as it is stated (Devarim 4:39): \"Know therefore this day, and take it to your heart, that the Lord is God in the heavens above and on the earth below; there is none else.\" And it is stated (Shemot 20:2): \"I am the Lord your God,\" and it is stated (Devarim 6:4): \"Hear, O Israel, the Lord is our God, the Lord is One.\" And it is stated (I Divrei Ha-yamim 28:9): \"Know the God of your father, and serve Him with a whole heart and with a willing soul.\" The study of faith is the study of the Divine revelation in the world, and not an attempt to understand His essence, which is beyond our comprehension. The study of the Divine revelation is the study of the words of the Torah and the prophets, which explain God's governance of the world, and also contemplation of creation, which is itself a Divine revelation.<sup>13</sup></p><p><b>In emotion</b>, through the mitzvot of loving and fearing God, as it is stated (Devarim 6:5): \"And you shall love the Lord your God with all your heart, and with all your soul, and with all your might,\" and it is stated (Devarim 10:20): \"You shall fear the Lord your God; Him shall you serve, and by His name shall you swear.\"</p><p><b>In all of life</b>, through the study of Torah and the fulfillment of all its mitzvot, as it is stated (Mishlei 3:6): \"In all your ways know Him.\" The Sages said (Berakhot 63a): \"This is a short passage upon which all the essentials of the Torah depend,\" because it contains the fundamental position of the faith of Israel, that faith must guide and elevate all realms of life.</p>","notes_en":[{"n":13,"en":"Although the mitzva of faith is above the ordinary mitzvot, the mitzva of \"I am\" is also one of the 613 mitzvot (Rambam, Hilkhot Yesodei Ha-Torah 1:6; Ḥinukh 25; Ramban, Shemot 20:1). Rabbi Ḥasdai Crescas raised a difficulty (introduction to his book Or Hashem): how is it possible to command faith? Surely a person must first believe, and only afterward can he be commanded! Therefore he held that faith is not one of the 613 mitzvot but precedes them. But according to those who hold that the mitzva of faith is one of the 613 mitzvot, there is a mitzva to study matters of faith as explained in the Torah, and to explain them in a manner that sits well with the intellect, according to tradition and logic, each person and generation according to its level and cultural environment. It must further be said that included in the mitzva of faith is to explain Divine governance and to cleanse it of every dross of corporealization or error that could lead to wickedness, foolishness or indifference, on the claim that such is the will of God (see Olat Re'aya, vol. 1, p. 336). Apparently, according to Rabbi Ḥasdai Crescas, these clarifications are included in the mitzva of Torah study."}]}]});
